From: FastDDS-dsoftbus-Transport standards work
Subject: [PATCH 0001] EDP: let a reader opt into its own type consistency QoS

Fast DDS 2.14.5 cannot match a DDS-XTypes 1.3 appendable or mutable type
against a reader that declares fewer members, whatever QoS the reader sets.

EDP::checkTypeIdentifier and EDP::checkTypeObject each construct a local
TypeConsistencyEnforcementQosPolicy fixed to DISALLOW_TYPE_COERCION with
m_prevent_type_widening true, and pass that to consistent() in place of
rdata->m_qos.type_consistency. The intended call is present in both
functions but commented out. CompleteStructType::consistent
(src/cpp/dynamic-types/TypeObject.cpp) already implements the assignable
case correctly, so only the policy plumbing is missing.

The commented-out call cannot simply be restored.
TypeConsistencyEnforcementQosPolicy defaults to ALLOW_TYPE_COERCION with
m_ignore_sequence_bounds and m_ignore_string_bounds true and
m_prevent_type_widening false, so honouring the reader's policy
unconditionally would relax endpoint matching for every reader that never
touched the policy, including readers created by rmw_fastrtps. That is a
behaviour change for existing deployments and is out of scope here.

This patch therefore gates the correct behaviour behind an environment
variable that defaults to off:

    FASTDDS_XTYPES_HONOR_TYPE_CONSISTENCY=1

With the variable unset, or set to anything other than exactly "1", every
matching decision is identical to stock 2.14.5. With it set to "1", a
reader's TypeConsistencyEnforcementQosPolicy reaches consistent() as
DDS-XTypes 1.3 specifies.

Scope notes, gate OFF:
  - No DDS or RTPS semantics change. Verified A/B on the host and on two
    RK3588A boards in both board directions: with the variable unset the
    patched library reproduces stock 2.14.5 case for case.
  - No transport-specific behaviour is introduced.

Scope notes, gate ON. The relaxation is WIDER than appendable/mutable
assignability, and an operator must understand all three points:

  - THE GATE IS PER-PROCESS, NOT PER-READER. It is a function-local static
    read once, so every reader in the process has its
    TypeConsistencyEnforcementQosPolicy honoured, not only the reader that
    wanted it. A process that sets the variable and also hosts rmw_fastrtps
    readers relaxes matching for those readers too. Do not set it in a shared
    launcher environment.

  - EXTENSIBILITY KIND IS NOT ENFORCED. In stock 2.14.5
    CompleteStructType::consistent (src/cpp/dynamic-types/TypeObject.cpp)
    compares m_struct_flags, which carry IS_FINAL / IS_APPENDABLE /
    IS_MUTABLE, only in operator==, never in consistent(); and
    CompleteStructHeader::consistent is effectively vacuous because
    CompleteTypeDetail::consistent returns true unconditionally. With
    DISALLOW_TYPE_COERCION the equal-size requirement masks this. Once the
    reader's default ALLOW policy is honoured, size no longer matters, so a
    reader declaring a FINAL struct can match a wider writer, which
    DDS-XTypes 1.3 forbids. This is upstream behaviour, not introduced here,
    but it is reachable ONLY when this gate is on.

  - A reader that deliberately sets DISALLOW_TYPE_COERCION is unaffected.
    Every other policy field that any consistent() overload reads is either
    guarded by m_kind == ALLOW_TYPE_COERCION or subsumed by the kind, and
    m_force_type_validation is never read inside consistent(). Member-ID
    equality and type-identifier consistency remain unconditional, so a
    genuinely incompatible member is still rejected.

  - This does not implement the XTypes assignability algorithm. It only
    stops the EDP from discarding the policy that selects it. The positional
    member comparison upstream fails closed, never open: a mutable reader
    omitting a middle member is rejected rather than wrongly matched.

Intended direction: an environment variable is the coarsest correct mechanism
for this. A participant PropertyPolicyQos property, or honouring the policy
only when the reader explicitly set a non-default one, would scope the opt-in
to the endpoint that asked for it and survive co-tenancy with rmw_fastrtps.

KNOWN DISCREPANCY between this header and the inserted source comment. The
doc comment added to EDP.cpp ends with "so no existing deployment or transport
changes behaviour". Read with its own preceding clause ("unless
FASTDDS_XTYPES_HONOR_TYPE_CONSISTENCY is exactly \"1\"") that is true of a
deployment which does not set the variable, but on its own it is broader than
the gate-ON scope notes above, and the independent review rejected it as a
standalone claim. It is deliberately left as it is: correcting it would change
the diff hunks, which would invalidate the libraries already built and
device-verified from this patch. Treat the gate-OFF and gate-ON scope notes in
this header, and docs/standards/ARCHITECTURE.md, as authoritative over that
sentence. Fold the correction into the next change that touches these hunks for
another reason.

Applies to: Fast DDS v2.14.5 (tag v2.14.5)
Verified with: git apply --check against a pristine v2.14.5 tree
---
--- a/src/cpp/rtps/builtin/discovery/endpoint/EDP.cpp
+++ b/src/cpp/rtps/builtin/discovery/endpoint/EDP.cpp
@@ -52,6 +52,8 @@
 
 #include <utils/collections/node_size_helpers.hpp>
 
+#include <cstdlib>
+#include <cstring>
 #include <mutex>
 
 using namespace eprosima::fastrtps;
@@ -73,6 +75,33 @@
     return partition.size() <= 1 && 0 == strlen(partition.name());
 }
 
+/**
+ * Whether endpoint type validation honours the reader's own
+ * TypeConsistencyEnforcementQosPolicy instead of the hardcoded strict policy.
+ *
+ * checkTypeIdentifier() and checkTypeObject() discard the reader's policy and
+ * substitute DISALLOW_TYPE_COERCION with m_prevent_type_widening, so a reader
+ * cannot opt into the type assignability that DDS-XTypes 1.3 defines, however
+ * it configures its QoS. The correct call is present in both functions but
+ * commented out.
+ *
+ * It cannot simply be enabled. TypeConsistencyEnforcementQosPolicy defaults to
+ * ALLOW_TYPE_COERCION with m_ignore_sequence_bounds and m_ignore_string_bounds
+ * true, so honouring it unconditionally would relax matching for every reader
+ * that never set the policy. This opt-in leaves the stock decision unchanged
+ * unless FASTDDS_XTYPES_HONOR_TYPE_CONSISTENCY is exactly "1", so no existing
+ * deployment or transport changes behaviour.
+ */
+static bool honor_reader_type_consistency()
+{
+    static const bool enabled = []() -> bool
+            {
+                const char* const value = std::getenv("FASTDDS_XTYPES_HONOR_TYPE_CONSISTENCY");
+                return value != nullptr && 0 == std::strcmp(value, "1");
+            }();
+    return enabled;
+}
+
 EDP::EDP(
         PDP* p,
         RTPSParticipantImpl* part)
@@ -1580,8 +1609,8 @@
     coercion.m_ignore_sequence_bounds = false;
     return wdata->type_id().m_type_identifier._d() != static_cast<uint8_t>(0x00) &&
            wdata->type_id().m_type_identifier.consistent(
-        //rdata->type_id().m_type_identifier, rdata->m_qos.type_consistency);
-        rdata->type_id().m_type_identifier, coercion);
+        rdata->type_id().m_type_identifier,
+        honor_reader_type_consistency() ? rdata->m_qos.type_consistency : coercion);
 }
 
 bool EDP::hasTypeIdentifier(
@@ -1637,8 +1666,8 @@
             coercion.m_force_type_validation = true;
             coercion.m_prevent_type_widening = true;
             coercion.m_ignore_sequence_bounds = false;
-            //return wtype->consistent(*rtype, rdata->m_qos.type_consistency);
-            return wtype->consistent(*rtype, coercion);
+            return wtype->consistent(*rtype,
+                            honor_reader_type_consistency() ? rdata->m_qos.type_consistency : coercion);
         }
 
         return false;
@@ -1663,8 +1692,8 @@
         coercion.m_force_type_validation = true;
         coercion.m_prevent_type_widening = true;
         coercion.m_ignore_sequence_bounds = false;
-        //return wdata->type().m_type_object.consistent(rdata->type().m_type_object, rdata->m_qos.type_consistency);
-        return wdata->type().m_type_object.consistent(rdata->type().m_type_object, coercion);
+        return wdata->type().m_type_object.consistent(rdata->type().m_type_object,
+                        honor_reader_type_consistency() ? rdata->m_qos.type_consistency : coercion);
     }
 
     return false;
